 "rationale": "The 6T Schmitt has strong feedback (M3/M6), which spreads the thresholds and gives >0.64 V hysteresis. A static crowbar current flows through M3-M1 and M4-M6 while vin is between the threshold and the rail. Long-L devices (L=2um) with near-minimum W keep that peak current at a few uA, so the average stays under 4.5 uA (8.1 uW). Positive feedback makes z switch fast, and the output inverter is purely static CMOS, so the output is rail-to-rail."}
